url https://pypi.org` → \"Could not resolve host\"). No GUI.\n- Pre-installed ML/data libraries: NONE.\n  - Missing (import raises ModuleNotFoundError): numpy, pandas, scipy, scikit-learn, torch, xgboost, matplotlib, seaborn, joblib, optuna, gpytorch, tensorflow.\n  - `pip list` shows only base-system packages; `pip install` cannot reach an index because the network is disabled.\n- Practical implication for any real run later: the environment would need fully offline wheel provisioning (none of which I will simulate here), or the experiment would need to be executed on a host with the required packages. Per the task definition this is a design-only deliverable, so this section is recorded for the researcher but does not block the design.\n\n3) Proposed approach — outline only (not the full design)\n- Target variable. Design explicitly accommodates either form. The public task says \"aerodynamic drag\" without fixing the variable; DrivAerNet++ dataset cards that I have seen in the literature release Cd and Cd·A separately, but no DrivAerNet++ document is supplied to this task, so the choice is left as an assumption (see item 4). The design will use one chosen target consistently and report results in that unit.\n- Candidate model architectures (5, chosen to span hypothesis class and training-cost extremes that make scaling differences visible):\n  1. Linear / Ridge regression (linear baseline; strong, cheap).\n  2. Random Forest regressor (`sklearn.ensemble.RandomForestRegressor`) — bagged trees.\n  3. Gradient Boosting / XGBoost regressor — boosted trees; if XGBoost is unavailable in the offline environment, fall back to `sklearn.ensemble.GradientBoostingRegressor`. If neither is available, fall back to `sklearn.ensemble.HistGradientBoostingRegressor`.\n  4. Gaussian Process regressor (`sklearn.gaussian_process.GaussianProcessRegressor` with an RBF/Matern kernel + feature standardization) — kernel method, gives native uncertainty.\n  5. MLP / small feed-forward neural network — either `sklearn.neural_network.MLPRegressor` (CPU-only) or a small PyTorch model; PyTorch is not currently installed.\n  Rationale for coverage: parametric → linear baseline, two nonparametric smooth/tree families that differ in inductive bias, a probabilistic kernel method, and a neural baseline. This set is small enough to fit the 24-CPU budget and diverse enough that architecture-dependent scaling, if it exists, will be detectable.\n- Training-set size sweep. A geometric grid spanning two decades, e.g. N ∈ {50, 100, 200, 400, 800, 1600} (truncated to 80% of the dataset size when the total is smaller). The densest points cluster where scaling is steepest. If the DrivAerNet++ parametric subset is small (the literature reports ~8k parametric designs in the original release, but again I am not citing a supplied document), the grid is rebased to roughly {10%, 20%, 35%, 50%, 75%, 100%} of unique designs, kept identical across models so each is evaluated on the same training indices.\n- Evaluation metric. Primary: normalized root-mean-square error on a held-out test set, `NRMSE = RMSE / (y_test.max() − y_test.min())`, plus absolute RMSE in the target's native unit as a secondary metric (so Cd vs. Cd·A scale is interpretable). For GP we additionally track mean negative log predictive density (NLPD) to capture calibration, not just accuracy.\n- Cross-validation / split strategy.\n  - Outer split: a single global train/test that is fixed across all N and all seeds, then nested sub-sampling inside the train pool. Test set is held out entirely from any model selection or fitting step.\n  - Stratification by body style (fastback / notchback / estateback) is included because the public task highlights body-style diversity as a feature of the data; without stratification, small N could fall on one body style and skew the comparison. The exact `body_style` column will be available in the parametric CSV.\n  - For each N and each model, repeated random subsampling of the train pool with K repeats (K = 5) and S seeds varying model stochasticity (S = 5), giving K·S = 25 replicates per (model, N) cell for the error bar.\n- Visualization of scaling. A single \"test error vs. N\" semi-log–log plot with one curve per model (x = N, y = mean test NRMSE ± 1 SE across K·S replicates, both axes log-scaled once the grid is geometric). A companion panel plots wall-clock fit time vs. N per model (illustrates the cost–accuracy tradeoff). A small-multiples grid (one model per panel) makes the per-architecture slope directly read off. A parity plot of predicted vs. true drag for the largest N summarizes accuracy.\n- Test for architecture differences. Two complementary comparisons, both performed at each N and globally:\n  1. Per-N: paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test across the K·S replicate errors for each pair of models, with Holm-Bonferroni correction across the 10 model pairs. Effect size: matched-pair rank-biserial correlation.\n  2. Across N: a linear mixed-effects model on log(RMSE) with fixed effects `log(N)` and `architecture` and their `log(N) × architecture` interaction, plus random intercept for replicate: a significant interaction term is the formal answer to \"does scaling differ across architectures?\". Residuals are checked.\n- Pre-registered expectations (not results): (a) test error decreases with N for every model; (b) the slope is steepest for GP / MLP and shallowest for the linear baseline; (c) at small N tree models typically beat the linear baseline, but the linear baseline catches up as N grows; (d) architecture differences are most visible between linear and nonparametric models and diminish with N.\n\n4) Assumptions and uncertainties\nEach line is flagged: [R] = reversible after researcher clarification; [F] = fixed by supplied documents (effectively none, because no dataset/track docs are present).\n\nA. Target variable identity and unit. The public task says \"aerodynamic drag\" but does not pin Cd vs. Cd·A or the unit.\n   - I assume: target = Cd (drag coefficient, dimensionless) for the primary analysis, because (i) the question literally says \"aerodynamic drag\" and the dataset card phrasing typically distinguishes \"Cd and Cd·A\" entries; (ii) NRMSE on a bounded dimensionless target is more interpretable. Cd·A (m²) will be reported as a secondary analysis if the dataset provides it. [R] — confirm.\n   - Unit: dimensionless for Cd; m² for Cd·A. [R].\n\nB. Inputs. The task states 26 parameters. I assume the parametric CSV contains exactly 26 numeric columns for the geometry, plus a `body_style` categorical column identifying fastback/notchback/estateback, plus a target column. Column names will be taken verbatim from the CSV header. [R].\n\nC. Sample count per body style and total. Not stated in any supplied document. I will not invent numbers; the sweep grid in item 3 will be rebased against the actual loaded CSV in any execution. [R].\n\nD. Train / val / test split. No supplied split. I will construct a fixed 80/10/10 stratified-by-body-style split once outside the sweep (test fully held out), then sub-sample only from the train pool for the N-grid, and use the val pool for any hyperparameter selection (small, fixed budget — see E). [R].\n\nE. Hyperparameter budget. Equalized across models to avoid unfair advantage. Plan: at each N, do a small inner validation using 25 trials of random search with a fixed seed and a hard cap of ≤ 60 s wall-clock per trial; selection criterion = validation NRMSE. For GP this caps at SMAC/MS/Matern length-scale search; for MLP it caps width/depth/lr/epochs; for trees it caps depth/min-leaf/n_estimators. The full hyperparameter space and selected values are logged. [R].\n\nF. Data leakage controls. (i) Standardization is fit on the training fold only and applied to val/test; (ii) any target transformation is fit on training residuals only; (iii) for RF/XGB no leakage since no target-side fitting of encoder; (iv) for trees, we do not use the target column as an input (sentinel check before fitting). The same physical sample never appears in both train and test (whole-design split, not per-row). [F by design].\n\nG. Repeated-seed protocol. Outer test split fixed. Inner sub-sampling uses S = 5 seeds × K = 5 subsamples = 25 replicates per (model, N). Mean and standard error are reported, plus median and IQR as robust checks. [R].\n\nH. Statistical test for \"architecture differs\". Primary: linear mixed model on log(RMSE) with fixed `log(N)`, `architecture`, `log(N):architecture` and random replicate intercept (see item 3). Significance threshold α = 0.01 given multiple Ns. Secondary: per-N paired Wilcoxon + Holm. Assumptions: residuals approximately normal — checked with Shapiro–Wilk; otherwise we fall back to a Friedman test across models at each N. [R].\n\nI. Body-style distribution. I assume the three styles are roughly balanced enough that stratified 80/10/10 is feasible without dropping a class; if a class has < 10% of total, the split becomes per-class 80/10/10 of that class (composite). [R].\n\nJ. Error-bar procedure. Bootstrap over the K·S replicates (B = 2000, seed fixed) for each (model, N) cell to obtain 95% CI on mean NRMSE. The line plot uses bootstrap CI, not analytic SE, because the replicate distribution is small (n = 25). [R].\n\nK. GP scalability. GP is O(N³); at the largest N in the sweep, exact GP may be infeasible without approximation. Plan: cap exact GP at N ≤ 2000 and use sparse / inducing-point GP (`sklearn` does not provide one natively, so the GP arm is truncated or replaced by the lazy `GaussianProcessRegressor`-with-subsample path — to be decided after seeing the dataset size). [R].\n\nL. Definition of \"training-set size improvement\". Operationalized as NRMSE decreasing monotonically with N for at least the central four grid points, plus a negative slope in the log-log regression of NRMSE on N per model. [R].\n\nM. Items fixed by supplied documents. None: no track.md content beyo