mensionless target is more interpretable. Cd\\u00b7A (m\\u00b2) will be reported as a secondary analysis if the dataset provides it. [R] \\u2014 confirm.\\n   - Unit: dimensionless for Cd; m\\u00b2 for Cd\\u00b7A. [R].\\n\\nB. Inputs. The task states 26 parameters. I assume the parametric CSV contains exactly 26 numeric columns for the geometry, plus a `body_style` categorical column identifying fastback/notchback/estateback, plus a target column. Column names will be taken verbatim from the CSV header. [R].\\n\\nC. Sample count per body style and total. Not stated in any supplied document. I will not invent numbers; the sweep grid in item 3 will be rebased against the actual loaded CSV in any execution. [R].\\n\\nD. Train / val / test split. No supplied split. I will construct a fixed 80/10/10 stratified-by-body-style split once outside the sweep (test fully held out), then sub-sample only from the train pool for the N-grid, and use the val pool for any hyperparameter selection (small, fixed budget \\u2014 see E). [R].\\n\\nE. Hyperparameter budget. Equalized across models to avoid unfair advantage. Plan: at each N, do a small inner validation using 25 trials of random search with a fixed seed and a hard cap of \\u2264 60 s wall-clock per trial; selection criterion = validation NRMSE. For GP this caps at SMAC/MS/Matern length-scale search; for MLP it caps width/depth/lr/epochs; for trees it caps depth/min-leaf/n_estimators. The full hyperparameter space and selected values are logged. [R].\\n\\nF. Data leakage controls. (i) Standardization is fit on the training fold only and applied to val/test; (ii) any target transformation is fit on training residuals only; (iii) for RF/XGB no leakage since no target-side fitting of encoder; (iv) for trees, we do not use the target column as an input (sentinel check before fitting). The same physical sample never appears in both train and test (whole-design split, not per-row). [F by design].\\n\\nG. Repeated-seed protocol. Outer test split fixed. Inner sub-sampling uses S = 5 seeds \\u00d7 K = 5 subsamples = 25 replicates per (model, N). Mean and standard error are reported, plus median and IQR as robust checks. [R].\\n\\nH. Statistical test for \\\"architecture differs\\\". Primary: linear mixed model on log(RMSE) with fixed `log(N)`, `architecture`, `log(N):architecture` and random replicate intercept (see item 3). Significance threshold \\u03b1 = 0.01 given multiple Ns. Secondary: per-N paired Wilcoxon + Holm. Assumptions: residuals approximately normal \\u2014 checked with Shapiro\\u2013Wilk; otherwise we fall back to a Friedman test across models at each N. [R].\\n\\nI. Body-style distribution. I assume the three styles are roughly balanced enough that stratified 80/10/10 is feasible without dropping a class; if a class has < 10% of total, the split becomes per-class 80/10/10 of that class (composite). [R].\\n\\nJ. Error-bar procedure. Bootstrap over the K\\u00b7S replicates (B = 2000, seed fixed) for each (model, N) cell to obtain 95% CI on mean NRMSE. The line plot uses bootstrap CI, not analytic SE, because the replicate distribution is small (n = 25). [R].\\n\\nK. GP scalability. GP is O(N\\u00b3); at the largest N in the sweep, exact GP may be infeasible without approximation. Plan: cap exact GP at N \\u2264 2000 and use sparse / inducing-point GP (`sklearn` does not provide one natively, so the GP arm is truncated or replaced by the lazy `GaussianProcessRegressor`-with-subsample path \\u2014 to be decided after seeing the dataset size). [R].\\n\\nL. Definition of \\\"training-set size improvement\\\". Operationalized as NRMSE decreasing monotonically with N for at least the central four grid points, plus a negative slope in the log-log regression of NRMSE on N per model. [R].\\n\\nM. Items fixed by supplied documents. None: no track.md content beyond the design-only one-liner, no files_manifest, no README, no dataset card, no DrivAerNet++ files. The only fixed facts are the workspace contents and the public-task prose quoted above. Everything in B\\u2013L is therefore assumption. [F].\\n\\nNo experiment has been executed; no outcome is claimed. Awaiting researcher review of items 1\\u20134 before producing the full design document.\", \"evidence\": \"Repository: only /workspace/task.json exists; broader search found no DrivAerNet++ files, no track.md, no README, no files_manifest anywhere on the filesystem. task.json `documents.track.md` is the one-line \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" and `datasets` is []. Verify: `ls -la /workspace`, `find /workspace -maxdepth 3`, `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname '*DrivAer*'` (no matches).\\nCompute: `python3 --version` \\u2192 3.12.3; `nproc` \\u2192 24; `nvidia-smi` fails (no GPU). Direct import of numpy, pandas, scipy, scikit-learn, torch, xgboost, matplotlib, seaborn, joblib, optuna, gpytorch, tensorflow all raise ModuleNotFoundError. `curl https://pypi.org` fails DNS \\u2192 network disabled. No preinstalled ML stack.\\nApproach outline: 5-model set (Ridge, RF, XGBoost/GradientBoosting fallback, GP-RBF, MLP), N \\u2208 {50,100,200,400,800,1600} (rebased to fractions if total smaller), metric = NRMSE (+ RMSE native units; + NLPD for GP), 80/10/10 stratified-by-body-style split, 25 replicates per cell (5 subsamples \\u00d7 5 seeds), log-log error-vs-N curve per model with bootstrap 95% CI, mixed-effects log(RMSE) ~ log(N) * architecture for the formal architecture test. (No execution.)\", \"assumptions\": [\"[R] Target variable = Cd (dimensionless); Cd\\u00b7A as secondary if available in the dataset.\", \"[R] 26 numeric parameter columns + one `body_style` categorical (fastback/notchback/estateback) + one target column; names taken from the CSV header at load time.\", \"[R] No supplied split; fixed 80/10/10 stratified-by-body-style outer split, subsampling only inside the train pool.\", \"[R] Equalized hyperparameter budget: \\u226425 random search trials, \\u226460 s each, criterion = validation NRMSE.\", \"[R] K = 5 sub-samples \\u00d7 S = 5 seeds = 25 replicates; bootstrap B = 2000 with fixed seed for error bars.\", \"[R] Statistical test = linear mixed model on log(RMSE) with log(N), architecture, log(N):architecture fixed effects and random replicate intercept; fallback Friedman if residuals non-normal.\", \"[R] GP arm truncated or replaced at large N if exact GP is infeasible (no sparse GP in sklearn).\", \"[R] \\\"Improvement with N\\\" defined as negative slope in log(RMSE) vs. log(N) with monotone decrease over the central four grid points.\", \"[R] Class-balance handling: per-class 80/10/10 if any body style is < 10% of total.\", \"[F] No DrivAerNet++ documents are supplied; therefore no count, split, column name, or unit is fixed by the inputs.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Exact target (Cd vs. Cd\\u00b7A) and unit \\u2014 task says \\\"aerodynamic drag\\\" but does not specify.\", \"Total sample size N_total and per-body-style counts \\u2014 no dataset in workspace.\", \"Whether a parametric CSV (or only meshes/STL) is the actual deliverable \\u2014 workspace is empty.\", \"Whether a held-out test split or body-style labels will be supplied at execution time.\", \"Whether offline ML packages will be installed before any real run (numpy/pandas/sklearn are missing, network is disabled).\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-bd0a4a9c-ed10-498b-a996-3e90af87a8a3", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 16}
{"id": "4a964a8b-5d52-4f3c-87b5-a47fe4f95829", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T18:44:05.917428", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "c26b5dc7-dde7-4845-818b-0ab855093e21", "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-b7a8567b-efd9-476a-b0d2-42375aaa3d90", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Handoff recorded; waiting for researcher review."}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "c26b5dc7-dde7-4845-818b-0ab855093e21", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 17}
{"id": "e4b89c15-42e6-43e1-8591-c55eacb56432", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T18:44:49.782080", "source": "user", "parent_id": "4a964a8b-5d52-4f3c-87b5-a47fe4f95829", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach approved. Produce the final experiment design document for the parametric-data surrogate-model scaling study on DrivAerNet++ aerodynamic drag. Required sections, all source-grounded where possible, and explicit about the design-only nature (no execution, no fabricated results): 1) Title, scope, and a one-paragraph research question restated exactly so the design is auditable against the project brief. 2) Inputs and target — explicitly state the default target assumption (Cd, dimensionless) and the conditional secondary analysis (Cd·A in m²), the assumed schema (26 numeric geometry parameters + a `body_style` categorical with values {fastback, notchback, estateback} + one target column), and how the schema would be read and validated from the parametric CSV at execution time. 3) Model architectures — five models (Ridge, RandomForest, XGBoost with sklearn GradientBoosting/HistGradientBoosting fallbacks in that order, GaussianProcess with standardized inputs and an RBF-or-Matern kernel, and a small MLP), with a one-line justification per model and a yes/no on whether each is feasible in the bubblewrap CPU-only environment given the missing libraries and disabled network. 4) Training-set-size sweep — the geometric grid {50, 100, 200, 400, 800, 1600} truncated to 80% of the loaded dataset, identical training indices across models at each N, and the rebasing rule used if N_total is smaller. 5) Controls and leakage safeguards — fixed 80/10/10 stratified-by-body-style outer split; per-class 80/10/10 composite if any class is <10%; standardization fit on train fold only; sentinel check that the target column is not an input; equalized per-model hyperparameter budget (≤25 random-search trials, ≤60 s/trial, criterion = validation NRMSE); no leakage of the held-out test set into any fitting or selection step. 6) Replication and error bars — K=5 sub-samples × S=5 seeds = 25 replicates per (model, N) cell; bootstrap B=2000 with a fixed seed for 95% CI on mean NRMSE; report median and IQR as robust checks; GP arm truncated or replaced if exact GP exceeds the cap (state explicit threshold, e.g. N>2000). 7) Metrics — primary NRMSE on the held-out test set; secondary RMSE in the target's native unit; GP-only NLPD. 8) Analysis plan — (a) log–log test-error-vs-N curve per model with bootstrap 95% CI; (b) wall-clock fit time vs. N for cost–accuracy tradeoff; (c) parity plot for the largest N; (d) formal test for architecture differences via a linear mixed-effects model on log(RMSE) with fixed effects log(N), architecture, log(N) × architecture and a random replicate intercept, fallback Friedman test if residuals fail Shapiro–Wilk; (e) per-N paired Wilcoxon across replicate errors with Holm-Bonferroni correction across model pairs and rank-biserial effect size; (f) operational definition of 'improvement with N' as monotone decrease of mean NRMSE across the central four grid points plus a negative log–log slope. 9) Feasibility — assess against the 24-CPU bubblewrap environment: state exactly which dependencies must be (offline) installed before any execution, which arm is GPU-dependent (none, in this design), the maximum wall-clock estimate per (model, N) cell under the bounded hyperparameter search, and the largest training size at which exact GP is feasible. 10) Pre-registered expectations (clearly labelled as such, not results): test error decreases with N for every model; slope steepest for GP/MLP and shallowest for the linear baseline; tree models beat the linear baseline at small N, the linear baseline catches up as N grows; architecture differences shrink with N. 11) Limitations and assumptions register — each of the eleven [R] items from the inspection report documented here verbatim as an assumption with its reversibility status, plus the explicit statement that no DrivAerNet++ files are present in /workspace and that no experiment has been executed. Do not invent sample counts, splits, units, column names, or results. Do not include code beyond pseudocode of the experimental loop. Deliver the document as a single self-contained Markdown file named `experiment_design.md` in /workspace, and report its path and section count when done.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 18}
{"id": "1c6d4cda-5600-45ad-8d5b-a9d7c196576f", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T18:45:59.986698", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "e4b89c15-42e6-43e1-8591-c55eacb56432", "action": null, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-0a22a131-7bbb-4dc3-b650-766a8e10db33", "tool_call": {"id": "call-0a22a131-7bbb-4dc3-b650-766a8e10db33", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Write experiment_design.md to workspace\",\"command\":\"cat > /workspace/experiment_design.md <<'MARKDOWN'\\n# Experiment Design — Parametric-Data Surrogate Models for Aerodynamic Drag on DrivAerNet++\\n\\n**Status:** Design-only document. No experiment has been executed. No DrivAerNet++ files are present in `/workspace` (see §11). This file is auditable against the public task brief; no fabricated sample counts, splits, column names, units, or results are included.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Title, Scope, and Research Question\\n\\n**Title.** A scaling and architecture comparison of parametric-data surrogate models for aerodynamic drag prediction on DrivAerNet++.\\n\\n**Scope.** This design specifies how to evaluate, on the 26-parameter DrivAerNet++ parametric subset (fastback / notchback / estateback), how the predictive performance of several surrogate model families changes as the training-set size is increased, and whether any architecture-dependence in that scaling exists.\\n\\n**Research question (restated verbatim from the project brief).** Does the predictive performance of surrogate models based on parametric data improve with increased training set size and differ across model architectures in aerodynamic drag prediction?\\n\\n**Non-goals.** No CFD execution, no mesh-based learning, no uncertainty-of-inputs analysis, no multi-fidelity modelling, no production model selection. This is a controlled benchmark of (a) scaling and (b) architecture on a single tabular predictive task.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Inputs and Target\\n\\n### 2.1 Default target assumption (primary)\\n- **Target variable.** Aerodynamic drag expressed as the drag coefficient **Cd** (dimensionless).\\n- This is the primary analysis target. The choice is fixed by default because the project brief refers to \\\"aerodynamic drag\\\" without specifying which released quantity is intended, and Cd is the dimensionless quantity under which normalized error metrics are most interpretable.\\n\\n### 2.2 Conditional secondary analysis\\n- If, at execution time, the parametric CSV also contains a Cd·A column (units of m²), a secondary analysis re-runs the §4 sweep on Cd·A and the §7 metrics are recomputed in their native units. The primary analysis is reported and the secondary analysis is reported only if the column exists; no results are invented if it does not.\\n\\n### 2.3 Assumed schema\\nAt load time the designer expects the parametric CSV to expose:\\n- **26 numeric geometry parameter columns** (one value per row, no NaN).\\n- **One categorical `body_style` column** with values in `{fastback, notchback, estateback}` (lowercase comparison; case-mapped at load).\\n- **One target column**: `Cd` (and, if present, `CdA`).\\n- **No NaN / Inf** in any input or target column; rows failing this check are dropped and counted.\\n\\n### 2.4 Read and validation procedure (execution-time pseudocode)\\n```\\nload parametric CSV\\nassert at least 26 numeric columns exist\\nidentify `body_style` (string dtype); coerce to {fastback, notchback, estateback}\\nidentify target column(s): primary = \\\"Cd\\\"; secondary = \\\"CdA\\\" if present\\nassert target has no NaN/Inf\\nfor each of 26 numeric inputs:\\n   assert dtype is numeric\\n   assert non-NaN\\n   correlation screen: drop inputs with |corr(target)| > 0.99 (sanity, not leakage — see §5)\\nlog per-class counts and feature names before any fitting\\n```\\n\\nColumn names are taken verbatim from the CSV header at load time and recorded in the run log; no hard-coded names appear anywhere in the analysis code besides `body_style`, `Cd`, and `CdA`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Model Architectures\\n\\nFive model families are compared so that both inductive bias and training-cost extremes are represented. Feasibility is given with respect to the bubblewrap environment as inspected (see §11 and §9).\\n\\n| # | Model | One-line justification | Feasible in bubblewrap (as inspected) |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | **Ridge regression** (linear baseline with L2 over standardized inputs) | Anchors the experiment to a closed-form low-capacity baseline; reveals how much of the signal is linearly recoverable. | **Yes**, if `scikit-learn` (preferred) or `numpy` alone is offline-installed. With raw `numpy.linalg.lstsq + np.linalg.norm` it is feasible even without sklearn. |\\n| 2 | **Random Forest regressor** (`sklearn.ensemble.RandomForestRegressor`) | Bagged trees; robust to monotonic feature transforms, gives a non-smooth second baseline. | **Yes** with offline-installed scikit-learn. CPU-only is fine. |\\n| 3 | **XGBoost** (`xgboost.XGBRegressor`, `tree_method=\\\"hist\\\"`) — **fallbacks in order**: (a) `sklearn.ensemble.GradientBoostingRegressor`, (b) `sklearn.ensemble.HistGradientBoostingRegressor` | Strongest tabular gradient-boosted baseline in the literature; representative of boosted trees. | XGBoost: **feasible only with offline wheel** (network disabled, package not preinstalled). sklearn GradientBoosting / HistGradientBoosting: **feasible** with offline-installed sklearn (Hist variant supports large N on CPU). |\\n| 4 | **Gaussian Process regressor** (`sklearn.gaussian_process.GaussianProcessRegressor`, RBF *or* Matérn-5/2 kernel, fixed `normalize_y=True`, `alpha` from hyperparameter search, inputs standardized beforehand) | Kernel method; provides native predictive variance and is the most training-data-hungry architecture in the panel, which exaggerates any architecture-specific scaling. | **Conditionally feasible.** Exact GP is `O(N³)`; feasible up to a published cap (see §6 and §9). |\\n| 5 | **Small MLP** — `sklearn.neural_network.MLPRegressor` (hidden layer {(64,), (128,64)}, ReLU, Adam) preferred; **fallback**: a tiny pure-NumPy two-layer NN with mini-batch SGD via `numpy` only. | Represents neural feature learning and exposes a different scaling slope from kernel and tree methods. | sklearn MLP: **feasible with offline-installed sklearn** on CPU. Pure-NumPy fallback: **feasible** even without any ML package. PyTorch is **not** installed and **cannot** be reached (network disabled), so PyTorch is *not* chosen as the primary MLP implementation. |\\n\\n**Decision rule at execution time.** The first option in the column above whose library is verified importable at the start of the run is selected. The same rule applies for the dataset loader: if `pandas` is missing, fall back to `numpy.genfromtxt`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Training-Set-Size Sweep\\n\\n### 4.1 Grid\\nThe architectural comparison uses a geometric grid that covers two orders of magnitude:\\n\\n```\\nN_grid = {50, 100, 200, 400, 800, 1600}\\n```\\n\\n- Each value represents the number of **unique parametric designs** in the training pool after the §5 outer split.\\n- Truncation rule: `N_grid = {n in N_grid | n <= floor(0.80 * N_total)}`. The factor `0.80` matches the §5 outer-train fraction.\\n\\n### 4.2 Identical training indices across models\\nFor every N ∈ `N_grid`, the same set of row indices is used to train **all five models**. This makes the per-N model comparison paired by training data and supports the §8 Wilcoxon and mixed-model tests.\\n\\n```\\nfor N in N_grid:\\n    training_indices, holdout_for_sub_eval = subsample(train_pool, N, seed=grid_seed)\\n    for model in five_models:\\n        for seed in seeds:\\n            model.fit(X[training_indices], y[training_indices], random_state=seed)\\n            evaluate on test_pool\\n            replicate → cell (model, N)\\n```\\n\\n### 4.3 Rebasing rule when N_total is smaller\\nIf `N_total` (after dropping rows with NaN/Inf and after the §5 outer split) is smaller than the smallest grid value, the grid is rebased to named **fractions** of `N_train = floor(0.80 * N_total)`:\\n\\n```\\nfraction_grid = {0.10, 0.20, 0.35, 0.50, 0.75, 1.00} of N_train\\nN_grid_rebased = sorted({floor(f * N_train) for f in fraction_grid} | {50})\\n```\\n\\nIf `N_train` is still below 50 (a sample-count regime this design does not target), N_grid is reduced to 6 evenly spaced quantiles below `N_train` and the §6 GP arm is dropped (see §6). The exact N values realized in any execution are recorded in the run log; **no number is hypothesized here**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Controls and Leakage Safeguards\\n\\n### 5.1 Outer split (fixed)\\n```\\nouter_train, outer_val, outer_test = stratified_split(\\n    full_data, fractions=(0.80, 0.10, 0.10), strata=\\\"body_style\\\", seed=SPLIT_SEED\\n)\\n```\\nThe outer split is **fixed once** at the top of the experiment and reused by every (model, N, replicate) combination. `outer_test` is never seen by any fitting or selection step.\\n\\n### 5.2 Composite split when a class is rare\\nIf any body style contains < 10% of `N_total`, the split becomes per-class 80/10/10 of that class, with the concatenated parts forming the global train/val/test. The class counts and the resulting split sizes are logged.\\n\\n### 5.3 Standardization\\n- Numeric inputs are standardized (`x' = (x − μ_train) / σ_train`) **fit on outer_train only** within each replicate. The same `(μ, σ)` are then applied to outer_val and outer_test.\\n- Target `y` is not standardized for primary metrics; for GP we additionally set `normalize_y=True` inside the model (regression on `y'` internally; predictions are back-transformed before scoring).\\n\\n### 5.4 Sentinel checks against target leakage\\nTwo checks, run before every fit:\\n1. The target column is **not** present in the design matrix.\\n2. No input column has `|corr(X_train, y_train)| > 0.99` after standardization. If any does, it is logged and dropped with reason (the parametric schema is unlikely to encode Cd directly, but this guards against accidental identity).\\n\\n### 5.5 Equalized hyperparameter budget\\n- A single `RandomizedSearchCV`-style routine is used for every model so the budget is materially identical.\\n- Per replicate (model, N): ≤ **25 trials** of random search, ≤ **60 s wall-clock per trial**, total cap of **25 × 60 s = 1500 s** of selection time per cell.\\n- Selection criterion: **validation NRMSE** (see §7), evaluated on `outer_val`.\\n- Final model after selection is re-fit on `outer_train ∪ outer_val` with the selected configuration before scoring on `outer_test`, **but only if** doing so does not re-leak the test set. The cost of this re-fit is included in the wall-clock budget (see §9).\\n\\n### 5.6 No leakage of the held-out test set\\n- Any cross-validation inside hyperparameter search uses only `outer_train` as folds.\\n- Bootstrap resampling for §6 and §8 is performed **on replicate errors**, not on raw rows.\\n- The split seed, hyperparameter seeds, and bootstrap seed are all logged.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Replication and Error Bars\\n\\n### 6.1 Replicates\\n- **K = 5** independent subsamples of `outer_train` per N (different row sets, fixed `grid_seed` plus a `sub_sample_id ∈ {0,…,4}`).\\n- **S = 5** model-stochasticity seeds per (model, N, subsample) (varied via `random_state` exposed by sklearn / XGBoost).\\n- **Total replicates per (model, N) cell: K·S = 25.**\\n\\n### 6.2 Error-bar procedure\\nFor each (model, N) cell:\\n1. Compute replicate metric values `m_1, …, m_25` on `outer_test`.\\n2. Draw **B = 2000** bootstrap resamples of `{m_i}` with replacement using `rng.default_rng(BOOT_SEED)`.\\n3. Report **mean metric**, **bootstrap 95% CI** (`[2.5%, 97.5%]` quantiles), **median**, and **IQR**.\\n4. The plot in §8a uses the bootstrap CI band (not analytic SE) because n = 25 per cell.\\n\\n### 6.3 GP cap and replacement rule\\n- **Threshold.** Exact GP is run for N ≤ 2000. Above that, the GP arm is **truncated** (no extrapolation reported) and a fallback `\\\"GP-sub\\\"` arm is reported instead: `sklearn.GaussianProcessRegressor` trained on a randomly chosen 2000-row subset of the training set (the subset is drawn once per replicate and logged). The decision threshold 2000 is the documented upper limit; it is **not** reinterpreted in execution.\\n- If at load time `N_total < 2000`, the GP cap is simply the largest N available and no fallback is needed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Metrics\\n\\n### 7.1 Primary\\n- **NRMSE (normalized root-mean-square error) on the held-out outer_test set**, defined as\\n  `NRMSE = sqrt(mean((ŷ − y)²)) / (max(y) − min(y))`.\\n\\n### 7.2 Secondary\\n- **RMSE in the native unit** (dimensionless for Cd; m² for Cd·A in the conditional secondary analysis). This is reported alongside NRMSE so the absolute error scale is interpretable.\\n\\n### 7.3 GP-specific\\n- **Negative log predictive density (NLPD)** at the predictive mean and variance that GP returns, computed on `outer_test`. NLPD rewards well-calibrated uncertainty and is reported as a GP-only chart (it is not part of the cross-model comparison).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Analysis Plan\\n\\n### 8.1 Visualizations\\n- **(a) Test-error-vs-N scaling curves.** For each model, plot **mean NRMSE vs. N** on log–log axes, with bootstrap 95% CI bands. The y-axis is inverted (lower is better). Six curves share a single figure; a `small_multiples` companion figure repeats with one model per panel so per-architecture slopes are directly read off.\\n- **(b) Cost–accuracy tradeoff.** Plot **wall-clock fit time vs. N** per model on log–log axes with median and IQR bands. A combined panel overlays accuracy and cost so the architecture regimes (e.g. GP expensive at large N, linear cheap but flat) are visible.\\n- **(c) Parity plot.** For the largest N in the grid, plot predicted vs. true drag, one marker per model, on `outer_test`. Annotate R², NRMSE, and RMSE per marker.\\n- **(d) Bootstrap CI on differences.** For each pair of models at each N, plot the bootstrap CI of `(NRMSE_A − NRMSE_B)`. CIs that exclude 0 are flagged as architecture-different at that N (informative companion to §8e).\\n\\n### 8.2 Formal test for architecture differences\\n- **Primary.** Linear mixed-effects model on `log(NRMSE)`:\\n  ```\\n  log(NRMSE) ~ log(N) + architecture + log(N):architecture\\n              + (1 | replicate_id)\\n  ```\\n  - `architecture` is a fixed factor with 5 levels; `log(N)` is fixed continuous; the interaction `log(N):architecture` is the formal test of *\\\"does scaling differ across architectures?\\\"*.\\n  - Fitted by REML; residual diagnostics include Shapiro–Wilk. If residuals are non-normal (p < 0.01), fall back to §8.3.\\n- **Secondary / per-N.** Per N, paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test across the 25 replicate errors for each of the 10 model pairs, with **Holm–Bonferroni** correction across pairs. Effect size: **matched-pair rank-biserial correlation**.\\n\\n### 8.3 Robustness fallback\\nIf the §8.2 mixed model residuals fail Shapiro–Wilk, we report **Friedman test** across the 5 models at each N (block = replicate_id), and a global Friedman across all (model, replicate, N) combinations with `log(N)` as a stratification factor.\\n\\n### 8.4 Operational definition of \\\"improvement with N\\\"\\nFor each model, success on the scaling claim is defined as the conjunction of:\\n1. **Monotone-decreasing** mean NRMSE across the **central four** points of the effective N_grid (the four interior points, excluding the smallest and largest).\\n2. **Negative slope** of an OLS fit of `log(NRMSE)` on `log(N)` per model, with the one-sided 95% CI on the slope excluding zero.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Feasibility in the Bubblewrap CPU-Only Environment\\n\\n### 9.1 Environment snapshot (as inspected, see §11)\\n- CPU: 24 cores. GPU: **none** (`nvidia-smi` cannot communicate with the driver).\\n- Python 3.12.3 available.\\n- Network: **disabled** (`curl https://pypi.org` fails DNS).\\n- No ML/data libraries preinstalled (numpy, pandas, scipy, scikit-learn, torch, xgboost, matplotlib, seaborn, joblib, optuna, gpytorch, tensorflow all import-fail).\\n- GUI: none.\\n\\n### 9.2 Dependencies needed before any execution (offline install required)\\nIn order of criticality:\\n1. **`numpy`** — required by everything else (and by the §9.4 NP-only fallbacks).\\n2. **`pandas`** — preferred for §2.4 schema validation; `numpy.genfromtxt` is the fallback.\\n3. **`scikit-learn`** — required for Models 1–4 and the preferred MLP; also provides `RandomizedSearchCV` for the §5.5 equalized budget.\\n4. **`scipy`** — required for Wilcoxon, Friedman, NLPD, Holm-Bonferroni.\\n5. **`xgboost`** — preferred for Model 3; sklearn GradientBoosting / HistGradientBoosting are the fallbacks.\\n6. **`matplotlib`** — required for §8.1 figures.\\n7. **`joblib`** — recommended for sklearn parallelism; not strictly required.\\n\\nAll of these must be **offline-installed** (wheels pre-staged in the bubblewrap) because `pip` cannot reach an index.\\n\\n### 9.3 GPU-dependent arms\\n**None.** This design is CPU-only by construction. Model 5 (MLP) is sized to fit comfortably on 24 CPU threads. Model 4 (GP) is the training-cost bottleneck but is not a GPU problem.\\n\\n### 9.4 Wall-clock budget per (model, N) cell\\nPer-cell cap = 1500 s of hyperparameter selection (25 trials × 60 s) + a final re-fit on `outer_train ∪ outer_val` + scoring on `outer_test`. Indicative orders of magnitude (CPU, not measured):\\n| Model | N = 50 | N = 200 | N = 800 | N = 1600 |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Ridge | << 60 s | << 60 s | << 60 s | << 60 s |\\n| Random Forest (≤ 500 trees) | ≤ 60 s | ≤ 300 s | ≤ 800 s | ≤ 1500 s |\\n| XGBoost / GradientBoosting | ≤ 60 s | ≤ 200 s | ≤ 600 s | ≤ 1200 s |\\n| GP (exact, Matérn) | ≤ 60 s | ≤ 600 s | ~ 30 min (cap-bound) | not run → GP-sub fallback |\\n| MLP (sklearn, ≤ 200 epochs, ≤ 128 hidden) | ≤ 60 s | ≤ 200 s | ≤ 800 s | ≤ 1500 s |\\n\\nThe total wall-clock consumption across the experiment is therefore bounded by: `|N_grid| × 5 models × K×S replicates × per-fit cost + selection cap`. Without measured N_total (no dataset present) the exact total cannot be quoted, but the per-cell cap of 1500 s makes an upper bound `6 × 5 × 25 × 1500 s = 1 125 000 s ≈ 13 days` of sequential computation, which is the worst case if every cell runs to the cap. In practice most cells finish well under cap and parallelism across the 24 cores can drag this down by roughly the number of cells run in parallel.\\n\\n### 9.5 Largest N at which exact GP is feasible\\n**N = 2000**, set in §6. The cap is set by the cubic cost plus the 1500 s per-cell hyperparameter budget, not by a numerical inversion issue.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Pre-Registered Expectations (NOT results)\\n\\nThese are explicitly hypotheses, recorded before any execution. They are **not** to be confused with outcomes.\\n\\n1. **Scaling.** Test NRMSE decreases with N for every model in the panel, with the largest improvement observed in the first decade of the grid.\\n2. **Slope ranking.** The log–log slope of NRMSE vs. N is **steepest for GP and MLP** (both capacity-limited at small N), **shallowest for the Ridge linear baseline** (capacity-bounded regardless of N).\\n3. **Cross-over.** At small N, **tree models (RF and XGBoost) outperform Ridge**, because their partition-based inductive bias handles non-linear geometry better in low-data regimes. As N grows, **Ridge narrows or closes the gap**, because the linear regime is asymptotically competitive for a 26-parameter continuous input space.\\n4. **Architecture-dependence diminishes with N.** The interaction term `log(N):architecture` in §8.2 is expected to be significant at small N and to weaken as N increases, consistent with classical learning-curve theory that well-specified models converge.\\n5. **GP variance calibration.** Even where GP is competitive in NRMSE, NLPD should worsen relative to MLP at the largest N because of the GP-sub fallback, not because GP fails.\\n6. **Cost.** Wall-clock fit time scales roughly linearly with N for Ridge and MLP, super-linearly for GP (cubic), and sub-linearly-per-tree but linearly-in-trees for RF and XGBoost.\\n\\nIf the realized run contradicts any of (1)–(6), the contradiction is to be reported, not absorbed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Limitations and Assumptions Register\\n\\n### 11.1 Dataset-presence limitation (fixed by inspection)\\n- **No DrivAerNet++ files are present in /workspace.** A broader filesystem search (`/workspace` and below) found no parametric CSV, no mesh files, no `track.md`, no `files_manifest.txt`, no `README`, and no dataset card.\\n- The only \\\"track\\\" content supplied is the one-line field inside `task.json`:\\n  > `track.md`: \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"\\n- The `datasets` list in the environment block is `[]`.\\n- **No experiment has been executed.** No outcome is claimed.\\n\\n### 11.2 Assumptions register (verbatim carry-over from the inspection report; each line tagged [R]eversible / [F]ixed)\\n\\n1. **[R] Target.** Primary target = Cd (dimensionless). Secondary target Cd·A (m²) is reported only if the parametric CSV exposes a `CdA` column at execution time. Reversible by the researcher's clarification of which DrivAerNet++ quantity is to be modelled.\\n2. **[R] Schema.** 26 numeric geometry parameter columns + one `body_style` (`{fastback, notchback, estateback}`) + one (or two) target column(s). Column names are read from the CSV header; no hard-coded names except `body_style`, `Cd`, and `CdA`. Reversible at load time.\\n3. **[R] Sample counts and per-body-style distribution.** Not stated in any supplied document. The sweep grid is rebased against the actual loaded dataset; no counts are hypothesized here. Reversible by disclosure of an actual DrivAerNet++ parametric CSV.\\n4. **[R] Train/val/test split.** No supplied split. The design constructs a fixed 80/10/10 stratified-by-body-style outer split, with subsampling only inside the outer-train pool. Reversible by supplying a DrivAerNet++ prescribed split.\\n5. **[R] Hyperparameter budget.** Equalized to ≤ 25 random-search trials, ≤ 60 s wall-clock per trial, criterion = validation NRMSE. Reversible by a tighter/laxer budget policy.\\n6. **[R] Repeated-seed protocol.** K = 5 sub-samples × S = 5 seeds = 25 replicates per (model, N); bootstrap B = 2000 with fixed seed; report mean, bootstrap 95% CI, median, IQR. Reversible by reseed policy.\\n7. **[R] Statistical test for \\\"architecture differs\\\".** Primary: linear mixed model on log(RMSE) with fixed effects `log(N)`, `architecture`, and `log(N):architecture`, random intercept for replicate; fallback Friedman if residuals fail Shapiro–Wilk. Reversible by adoption of a different test family.\\n8. **[R] Body-style distribution handling.** Composite per-class 80/10/10 if any class is < 10% of total. Reversible by changing the imbalance threshold.\\n9. **[R] Error-bar procedure.** Bootstrap over 25 replicate errors with B = 2000, fixed seed. Reversible by adopting analytic SE or a different bootstrap design.\\n10. **[R] GP scalability.** Cap of exact GP at N = 2000; fallback GP-sub (random 2000-row subset) above the cap. Reversible by adopting a sparse/inducing-point GP if offline-installed.\\n11. **[R] Definition of \\\"improvement with N\\\".** Monotone-decreasing mean NRMSE across the central four grid points **and** negative one-sided 95% CI slope in `log(NRMSE) ~ log(N)`. Reversible by adopting a stricter or weaker criterion.\\n12. **[F] Fixed by the supplied documents.** The only fixed facts are (i) the workspace contents (no DrivAerNet++ files), (ii) the one-line `track.md` field embedded in `task.json`, and (iii) the public-task prose quoted in §1. All quantitative details in §2.1, §2.2, §2.3, §3, §4, §5, §6, §7, §8.4, §9.4, and §9.5 are assumption, not evidence.\\n\\n### 11.3 Cross-cutting limitations\\n- **No execution was attempted.** This file is a design document; its outputs are analyses-of-design, not analyses-of-data.\\n- **Offline tooling requirement.** Without offline-installed numpy / pandas / scikit-learn / scipy / xgboost / matplotlib, no arm of this design can be exercised in the bubblewrap environment as inspected.\\n- **Sample-size sensitivity.** Conclusions about GP and MLP scaling depend on whether N_total reaches ≥ 1600 effectively. If not, the §4.3 rebasing rule applies and the §6 GP arm is reduced or replaced.\\n- **No architecture-search.** The five models are fixed a priori; no neural architecture search is performed, so MLP conclusions are conditional on the chosen hidden-layer sizes.\\n- **Bootstrap CIs on differences.** §8.1(d) assumes the bootstrap distribution of `(NRMSE_A − NRMSE_B)` is symmetric; this is checked at execution time and a percentile or bias-corrected variant may be reported instead.\\nMARKDOWN\\necho \\\"wrote experiment_design.md\\\"\\nwc -l /workspace/experiment_design.md\\nls -la /workspace/experiment_design.md\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-f7cf5168-6bec-4c23-9e4a-d393a17ffb3c", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 19}
{"id": "2fe738f8-fa25-436b-a15f-580fab8734a6", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T18:45:59.987133", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "1c6d4cda-5600-45ad-8d5b-a9d7c196576f", "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-0a22a131-7bbb-4dc3-b650-766a8e10db33", "error": "Error validating tool 'execute_check': 1 validation error for ExecuteCheckAction\ncommand\n  String should have at most 12000 characters [type=string_too_long, input_value='cat > /workspace/experim...ce/experiment_design.md', input_type=str]\n    For further information visit https://errors.pydantic.dev/2.13/v/string_too_long. Parameters provided: ['command']", "classification": {"kind": "agent_action", "retryable": true, "user_action": "retry", "error_id": null}, "kind": "AgentErrorEvent", "source_event_index": 20}
{"id": "7c433c9d-e496-4cde-88d3-88347bfc632a", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T18:46:18.507502", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "2fe738f8-fa25-436b-a15f-580fab8734a6", "action": {"command": "cat > /workspace/experiment_design.md <<'MARKDOWN'\n# Experiment Design — Parametric-Data Surrogate Models for Aerodynamic Drag on DrivAerNet++\n\n**Status:** Design-only document. No experiment has been executed. No DrivAerNet++ files are present in `/workspace` (see §11). This file is auditable against the public task brief; no fabricated sample counts, splits, column names, units, or results are included.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Title, Scope, and Research Question\n\n**Title.** A scaling and architecture comparison of parametric-data surrogate models for aerodynamic drag prediction on DrivAerNet++.\n\n**Scope.** This design specifies how to evaluate, on the 26-parameter DrivAerNet++ parametric subset (fastback / notchback / estateback), how the predictive performance of several surrogate model families changes as the training-set size is increased, and whether any architecture-dependence in that scaling exists.\n\n**Research question (restated verbatim from the project brief).** Does the predictive performance of surrogate models based on parametric data improve with increased training set size and differ across model architectures in aerodynamic drag prediction?\n\n**Non-goals.** No CFD execution, no mesh-based learning, no uncertainty-of-inputs analysis, no multi-fidelity modelling, no production model selection. This is a controlled benchmark of (a) scaling and (b) architecture on a single tabular predictive task.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Inputs and Target\n\n### 2.1 Default target assumption (primary)\n- **Target variable.** Aerodynamic drag expressed as the drag coefficient **Cd** (dimensionless).\n- This is the primary analysis target. The choice is fixed by default because the project brief refers to \"aerodynamic drag\" without specifying which released quantity is intended, and Cd is the dimensionless quantity under which normalized error metrics are most interpretable.\n\n### 2.2 Conditional secondary analysis\n- If, at execution time, the parametric CSV also contains a Cd·A column (units of m²), a secondary analysis re-runs the §4 sweep on Cd·A and the §7 metrics are recomputed in their native units. The primary analysis is reported and the secondary analysis is reported only if the column exists; no results are invented if it does not.\n\n### 2.3 Assumed schema\nAt load time the designer expects the parametric CSV to expose:\n- **26 numeric geometry parameter columns** (one value per row, no NaN).\n- **One categorical `body_style` column** with values in `{fastback, notchback, estateback}` (lowercase comparison; case-mapped at load).\n- **One target column**: `Cd` (and, if present, `CdA`).\n- **No NaN / Inf** in any input or target column; rows failing this check are dropped and counted.\n\n### 2.4 Read and validation procedure (execution-time pseudocode)\n```\nload parametric CSV\nassert at least 26 numeric columns exist\nidentify `body_style` (string dtype); coerce to {fastback, notchback, estateback}\nidentify target column(s): primary = \"Cd\"; secondary = \"CdA\" if present\nassert target has no NaN/Inf\nfor each of 26 numeric inputs:\n   assert dtype is numeric\n   assert non-NaN\n   correlation screen: drop inputs with |corr(target)| > 0.99 (sanity, not leakage — see §5)\nlog per-class counts and feature names before any fitting\n```\n\nColumn names are taken verbatim from the CSV header at load time and recorded in the run log; no hard-coded names appear anywhere in the analysis code besides `body_style`, `Cd`, and `CdA`.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Model Architectures\n\nFive model families are compared so that both inductive bias and training-cost extremes are represented. Feasibility is given with respect to the bubblewrap environment as inspected (see §11 and §9).\n\n| # | Model | One-line justification | Feasible in bubblewrap (as inspected) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Ridge regression** (linear baseline with L2 over standardized inputs) | Anchors the experiment to a closed-form low-capacity baseline; reveals how much of the signal is linearly recoverable. | **Yes**, if `scikit-learn` (preferred) or `numpy` alone is offline-installed. With raw `numpy.linalg.lstsq + np.linalg.norm` it is feasible even without sklearn. |\n| 2 | **Random Forest regressor** (`sklearn.ensemble.RandomForestRegressor`) | Bagged trees; robust to monotonic feature transforms, gives a non-smooth second baseline. | **Yes** with offline-installed scikit-learn. CPU-only is fine. |\n| 3 | **XGBoost** (`xgboost.XGBRegressor`, `tree_method=\"hist\"`) — **fallbacks in order**: (a) `sklearn.ensemble.GradientBoostingRegressor`, (b) `sklearn.ensemble.HistGradientBoostingRegressor` | Strongest tabular gradient-boosted baseline in the literature; representative of boosted trees. | XGBoost: **feasible only with offline wheel** (network disabled, package not preinstalled). sklearn GradientBoosting / HistGradientBoosting: **feasible** with offline-installed sklearn (Hist variant supports large N on CPU). |\n| 4 | **Gaussian Process regressor** (`sklearn.gaussian_process.GaussianProcessRegressor`, RBF *or* Matérn-5/2 kernel, fixed `normalize_y=True`, `alpha` from hyperparameter search, inputs standardized beforehand) | Kernel method; provides native predictive variance and is the most training-data-hungry architecture in the panel, which exaggerates any architecture-specific scaling. | **Conditionally feasible.** Exact GP is `O(N³)`; feasible up to a published cap (see §6 and §9). |\n| 5 | **Small MLP** — `sklearn.neural_network.MLPRegressor` (hidden layer {(64,), (128,64)}, ReLU, Adam) preferred; **fallback**: a tiny pure-NumPy two-layer NN with mini-batch SGD via `numpy` only. | Represents neural feature learning and exposes a different scaling slope from kernel and tree methods. | sklearn MLP: **feasible with offline-installed sklearn** on CPU. Pure-NumPy fallback: **feasible** even without any ML package. PyTorch is **not** installed and **cannot** be reached (network disabled), so PyTorch is *not* chosen as the primary MLP implementation. |\n\n**Decision rule at execution time.** The first option in the column above whose library is verified importable at the start of the run is selected. The same rule applies for the dataset loader: if `pandas` is missing, fall back to `numpy.genfromtxt`.\n\nMARKDOWN\necho \"section 1-3 written\"\nwc -l /workspace/experiment_design.md", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-b31b0608-5b7b-444e-a55e-a7aa86fa29c0", "tool_call": {"id": "call-b31b0608-5b7b-444e-a55e-a7aa86fa29c0", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Write first chunk of experiment_design.md\", \"command\": \"cat > /workspace/experiment_design.md <<'MARKDOWN'\\n# Experiment Design \\u2014 Parametric-Data Surrogate Models for Aerodynamic Drag on DrivAerNet++\\n\\n**Status:** Design-only document. No experiment has been executed. No DrivAerNet++ files are present in `/workspace` (see \\u00a711). This file is auditable against the public task brief; no fabricated sample counts, splits, column names, units, or results are included.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Title, Scope, and Research Question\\n\\n**Title.** A scaling and architecture comparison of parametric-data surrogate models for aerodynamic drag prediction on DrivAerNet++.\\n\\n**Scope.** This design specifies how to evaluate, on the 26-parameter DrivAerNet++ parametric subset (fastback / notchback / estateback), how the predictive performance of several surrogate model families changes as the training-set size is increased, and whether any architecture-dependence in that scaling exists.\\n\\n**Research question (restated verbatim from the project brief).** Does the predictive performance of surrogate models based on parametric data improve with increased training set size and differ across model architectures in aerodynamic drag prediction?\\n\\n**Non-goals.** No CFD execution, no mesh-based learning, no uncertainty-of-inputs analysis, no multi-fidelity modelling, no production model selection. This is a controlled benchmark of (a) scaling and (b) architecture on a single tabular predictive task.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Inputs and Target\\n\\n### 2.1 Default target assumption (primary)\\n- **Target variable.** Aerodynamic drag expressed as the drag coefficient **Cd** (dimensionless).\\n- This is the primary analysis target. The choice is fixed by default because the project brief refers to \\\"aerodynamic drag\\\" without specifying which released quantity is intended, and Cd is the dimensionless quantity under which normalized error metrics are most interpretable.\\n\\n### 2.2 Conditional secondary analysis\\n- If, at execution time, the parametric CSV also contains a Cd\\u00b7A column (units of m\\u00b2), a secondary analysis re-runs the \\u00a74 sweep on Cd\\u00b7A and the \\u00a77 metrics are recomputed in their native units. The primary analysis is reported and the secondary analysis is reported only if the column exists; no results are invented if it does not.\\n\\n### 2.3 Assumed schema\\nAt load time the designer expects the parametric CSV to expose:\\n- **26 numeric geometry parameter columns** (one value per row, no NaN).\\n- **One categorical `body_style` column** with values in `{fastback, notchback, estateback}` (lowercase comparison; case-mapped at load).\\n- **One target column**: `Cd` (and, if present, `CdA`).\\n- **No NaN / Inf** in any input or target column; rows failing this check are dropped and counted.\\n\\n### 2.4 Read and validation procedure (execution-time pseudocode)\\n```\\nload parametric CSV\\nassert at least 26 numeric columns exist\\nidentify `body_style` (string dtype); coerce to {fastback, notchback, estateback}\\nidentify target column(s): primary = \\\"Cd\\\"; secondary = \\\"CdA\\\" if present\\nassert target has no NaN/Inf\\nfor each of 26 numeric inputs:\\n   assert dtype is numeric\\n   assert non-NaN\\n   correlation screen: drop inputs with |corr(target)| > 0.99 (sanity, not leakage \\u2014 see \\u00a75)\\nlog per-class counts and feature names before any fitting\\n```\\n\\nColumn names are taken verbatim from the CSV header at load time and recorded in the run log; no hard-coded names appear anywhere in the analysis code besides `body_style`, `Cd`, and `CdA`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Model Architectures\\n\\nFive model families are compared so that both inductive bias and training-cost extremes are represented. Feasibility is given with respect to the bubblewrap environment as inspected (see \\u00a711 and \\u00a79).\\n\\n| # | Model | One-line justification | Feasible in bubblewrap (as inspected) |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | **Ridge regression** (linear baseline with L2 over standardized inputs) | Anchors the experiment to a closed-form low-capacity baseline; reveals how much of the signal is linearly recoverable. | **Yes**, if `scikit-learn` (preferred) or `numpy` alone is offline-installed. With raw `numpy.linalg.lstsq + np.linalg.norm` it is feasible even without sklearn. |\\n| 2 | **Random Forest regressor** (`sklearn.ensemble.RandomForestRegressor`) | Bagged trees; robust to monotonic feature transforms, gives a non-smooth second baseline. | **Yes** with offline-installed scikit-learn. CPU-only is fine. |\\n| 3 | **XGBoost** (`xgboost.XGBRegressor`, `tree_method=\\\"hist\\\"`) \\u2014 **fallbacks in order**: (a) `sklearn.ensemble.GradientBoostingRegressor`, (b) `sklearn.ensemble.HistGradientBoostingRegressor` | Strongest tabular gradient-boosted baseline in the literature; representative of boosted trees. | XGBoost: **feasible only with offline wheel** (network disabled, package not preinstalled). sklearn GradientBoosting / HistGradientBoosting: **feasible** with offline-installed sklearn (Hist variant supports large N on CPU). |\