ctor with 5 levels; `log(N)` is fixed continuous; the interaction `log(N):architecture` is the formal test of *\\\"does scaling differ across architectures?\\\"*.\\n  - Fitted by REML; residual diagnostics include Shapiro\\u2013Wilk. If residuals are non-normal (p < 0.01), fall back to \\u00a78.3.\\n\\n### 8.3 Robustness fallback\\nIf the \\u00a78.2 mixed model residuals fail Shapiro\\u2013Wilk, we report **Friedman test** across the 5 models at each N (block = `replicate_id`), and a global Friedman across all (model, replicate, N) combinations with `log(N)` as a stratification factor.\\n\\n### 8.4 Per-N paired comparisons and effect sizes\\nPer N, paired **Wilcoxon signed-rank test** across the 25 replicate errors for each of the 10 model pairs, with **Holm\\u2013Bonferroni** correction across pairs. Effect size: **matched-pair rank-biserial correlation**.\\n\\n### 8.5 Operational definition of \\\"improvement with N\\\"\\nFor each model, success on the scaling claim is defined as the conjunction of:\\n1. **Monotone-decreasing** mean NRMSE across the **central four** points of the effective N_grid (the four interior points, excluding the smallest and largest).\\n2. **Negative slope** of an OLS fit of `log(NRMSE)` on `log(N)` per model, with the one-sided 95% CI on the slope excluding zero.\\n\\nMARKDOWN\\necho \\\"section 7-8 appended\\\"\\nwc -l /workspace/experiment_design.md\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-1a816c58-5d96-4603-a9b8-7d562f628f76", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 25}
