{"id": "ababf231-485a-4a8f-a20f-3bed5c86170d", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T18:47:14.137659", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "d3e5aa9e-8646-43a2-bd36-57feafca65e7", "action": {"command": "cat >> /workspace/experiment_design.md <<'MARKDOWN'\n\n---\n\n## 10. Pre-Registered Expectations (NOT results)\n\nThese are explicitly hypotheses, recorded before any execution. They are **not** to be confused with outcomes. If a realized run contradicts any of items 1–6, the contradiction is to be reported, not absorbed.\n\n1. **Scaling.** Test NRMSE decreases with N for every model in the panel, with the largest improvement observed in the first decade of the grid.\n2. **Slope ranking.** The log–log slope of NRMSE vs. N is **steepest for GP and MLP** (both capacity-limited at small N), **shallowest for the Ridge linear baseline** (capacity-bounded regardless of N).\n3. **Cross-over.** At small N, **tree models (RF and XGBoost) outperform Ridge**, because their partition-based inductive bias handles non-linear geometry better in low-data regimes. As N grows, **Ridge narrows or closes the gap**, because the linear regime is asymptotically competitive for a 26-parameter continuous input space.\n4. **Architecture-dependence diminishes with N.** The interaction term `log(N):architecture` in §8.2 is expected to be significant at small N and to weaken as N increases, consistent with classical learning-curve theory that well-specified models converge.\n5. **GP variance calibration.** Even where GP is competitive in NRMSE, NLPD relative to MLP is expected to remain stable up to the N = 2000 cap; above that, the GP-sub fallback will likely make NLPD worse than for a fixed-budget exact GP, while NRMSE will be similar.\n6. **Cost.** Wall-clock fit time scales roughly linearly with N for Ridge and MLP, super-linearly (cubically) for GP, and roughly linearly in `n_estimators × min_sample_split^{-1}` for RF and XGBoost.\n\nMARKDOWN\necho \"section 10 appended\"\nwc -l /workspace/experiment_design.md", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-156023b0-9614-4055-95f2-a59ff36830fd", "tool_call": {"id": "call-156023b0-9614-4055-95f2-a59ff36830fd", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Append section 10 to experiment_design.md\", \"command\": \"cat >> /workspace/experiment_design.md <<'MARKDOWN'\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Pre-Registered Expectations (NOT results)\\n\\nThese are explicitly hypotheses, recorded before any execution. They are **not** to be confused with outcomes. If a realized run contradicts any of items 1\\u20136, the contradiction is to be reported, not absorbed.\\n\\n1. **Scaling.** Test NRMSE decreases with N for every model in the panel, with the largest improvement observed in the first decade of the grid.\\n2. **Slope ranking.** The log\\u2013log slope of NRMSE vs. N is **steepest for GP and MLP** (both capacity-limited at small N), **shallowest for the Ridge linear baseline** (capacity-bounded regardless of N).\\n3. **Cross-over.** At small N, **tree models (RF and XGBoost) outperform Ridge**, because their partition-based inductive bias handles non-linear geometry better in low-data regimes. As N grows, **Ridge narrows or closes the gap**, because the linear regime is asymptotically competitive for a 26-parameter continuous input space.\\n4. **Architecture-dependence diminishes with N.** The interaction term `log(N):architecture` in \\u00a78.2 is expected to be significant at small N and to weaken as N increases, consistent with classical learning-curve theory that well-specified models converge.\\n5. **GP variance calibration.** Even where GP is competitive in NRMSE, NLPD relative to MLP is expected to remain stable up to the N = 2000 cap; above that, the GP-sub fallback will likely make NLPD worse than for a fixed-budget exact GP, while NRMSE will be similar.\\n6. **Cost.** Wall-clock fit time scales roughly linearly with N for Ridge and MLP, super-linearly (cubically) for GP, and roughly linearly in `n_estimators \\u00d7 min_sample_split^{-1}` for RF and XGBoost.\\n\\nMARKDOWN\\necho \\\"section 10 appended\\\"\\nwc -l /workspace/experiment_design.md\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-a1143db6-e7bd-4df7-a961-ead2d9c36cab", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 29}
